      "math_academy_id": "q-129271",
      "sequence_position": 14,
      "problem": "Calculate \n${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨15,\\,3,\\,6⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{2}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨12,\\,2,\\,4⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the adjacent intervals rule, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{1}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nFinally, using the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨15,\\,3,\\,6⟩-⟨12,\\,2,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,1,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
